/* book Worked example: Q: The engine of the CI chapter (2000 rev/min, injection from 15° bTDC at 1.6 mg/°) runs on two fuels. Fuel A gives a 0.9 ms delay; fuel B, of higher cetane number, gives 0.5 ms. For each, find the delay in degrees, the start of combustion and the fuel injected before ignition. The computed traces in the figure give a steepest rise of 8.8 bar/°CA for A and 6.3 bar/°CA for B. Comment.
   1. Degrees per ms at 2000 rev/min: 0.006 × 2000 = 12 °/ms (the 6 is 360° ÷ 60 s, the 0.001 turns ms into s).
   2. Fuel A: 12 × 0.9 = 10.8°; combustion starts at −15° + 10.8° = 4.2° bTDC; fuel before ignition 1.6 × 10.8 = 17.3 mg.
   3. Fuel B: 12 × 0.5 = 6.0°; combustion starts at −15° + 6° = 9° bTDC; fuel before ignition 1.6 × 6.0 = 9.6 mg.
   4. Fuel B halves the fuel waiting at ignition (9.6 against 17.3 mg), so its premixed spike is smaller and the steepest rise falls from 8.8 to 6.3 bar/°CA.
   5. Fuel B starts burning earlier, so its peak pressure is a little higher (86 against 80 bar) even though it runs more smoothly: peak pressure alone does not measure knock.
   Answer: A: 10.8°, 4.2° bTDC, 17.3 mg. B: 6.0°, 9° bTDC, 9.6 mg. The higher-cetane fuel cuts the fuel collected before ignition by 45 % and the steepest pressure rise by 29 %. */
