/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A CI engine has only about 1 to 2 ms to mix its fuel with air, and the fuel arrives as a few narrow sprays into a chamber full of air.
   - Organised motion and turbulence are different things.
   - Swirl is rotation of the charge about the cylinder axis.
   - Swirl persists through compression, losing some energy to wall friction.
   - Squish is the radial inward flow created when the piston crown comes very close to a flat part of the head, the squish band or squish area, near TDC.
   - Tumble is rotation about an axis at right angles to the cylinder axis, so the charge rolls over like a barrel.
   - In short: diesels rely mainly on swirl and squish; petrol engines mainly on tumble and squish.
*/
