/* book Worked example: Q: A DI diesel has bore 100 mm and stroke 110 mm. Its piston bowl is 60 mm across and 16 mm deep (take it as a cylinder). The squish gap at TDC is 1.0 mm over the full bore, and valve cut-outs and crevices add 0.90 cm³. Find the clearance volume, the compression ratio and the share of the clearance volume held by the bowl.
   1. Bore area = (π/4) × 10² = 78.54 cm². Swept volume Vs = 78.54 × 11.0 = 863.9 cm³.
   2. Bowl volume = (π/4) × 6.0² × 1.6 = 28.27 × 1.6 = 45.24 cm³.
   3. Squish gap volume = 78.54 × 0.10 = 7.85 cm³.
   4. Vc = 45.24 + 7.85 + 0.90 = 53.99 cm³.
   5. r = (863.9 + 53.99) ÷ 53.99 = 17.0.
   6. Bowl share = 45.24 ÷ 53.99 = 0.838, about 84 %.
   Answer: Vc = 54.0 cm³ and r = 17.0; the bowl holds about 84 % of the clearance volume, which is why nearly all the air is in the bowl, swirling, when the fuel arrives. */
