/* book Worked example 2: Q: An SI engine has bore 86 mm and clearance volume 62.44 cm³. Compare the surface-to-volume ratio of (a) a flat disc chamber between a flat head and a flat piston and (b) a dome (spherical cap) over a flat piston, both of 62.44 cm³.
   1. Piston area = π × 4.3² = 58.09 cm².
   2. (a) Disc height = 62.44 ÷ 58.09 = 1.075 cm. Surface = head + piston + side = 2 × 58.09 + 2π × 4.3 × 1.075 = 116.18 + 29.04 = 145.2 cm². S/V = 145.2 ÷ 62.44 = 2.33 cm⁻¹.
   3. (b) A cap of base radius a = 4.3 cm and height h has V = πh(3a² + h²)/6. Setting V = 62.44 cm³ and solving gives h = 2.00 cm (check: π × 2.00 × (55.47 + 4.02)/6 = 62.3 ≈ 62.44 cm³).
   4. Dome area = π(a² + h²) = π(18.49 + 4.02) = 70.7 cm²; with the piston, S = 70.7 + 58.09 = 128.8 cm². S/V = 128.8 ÷ 62.44 = 2.06 cm⁻¹.
   5. The dome has 11 % less surface for the same volume.
   Answer: (a) 2.33 cm⁻¹. (b) 2.06 cm⁻¹. The dome loses less heat and has less quench area, one reason hemispherical and pent-roof heads burn efficiently. */
